    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Christian Yelich** and **Yordan Alvarez** pacing the offense as our primary anchors. **Christian Yelich** earns the top efficiency score of 70.50, benefitting from a favorable matchup against Zebby Matthews; his profile is bolstered significantly by a dynamic platoon advantage and a slow opposing delivery that screams potential for a stolen base, keeping him in lockstep with our organizational mandate to maximize speed. Similarly, **Yordan Alvarez** (69.65) is a must-start despite the tough matchup against Robbie Ray, as his elite 0.473 xwOBA overrides the slight negative impact of a same-handed platoon. **Trea Turner** (64.41) and **Kyle Schwarber** (66.09) also project as strong plays, providing the power-speed upside necessary for a competitive Roto day, though both carry exposure to the 33% rain risk in their respective games.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved the final outfield spot and utility role, where **Pete Crow-Armstrong** was the odd man out with a score of 54.25. While **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits from an elite spot at the top of the order, the optimizer favored **Carson Benge** (58.08) and **Lars Nootbaar** (57.75) due to superior platoon splits and better matchup context against Carmen Mlodzinski and Roki Sasaki, respectively. Elsewhere, **Kazuma Okamoto** faces a gauntlet against Zack Wheeler; while his 45.11 score is marginal, his presence in the heart of the order remains essential for our RBI production despite the 2.85 xERA staring him down. We continue to monitor the status of **Casey Schmitt**, who remains sidelined on the IL, while keeping **Josh Smith** on the bench due to his struggles against high-end pitching and the defensive hurdles presented by his opponent's catchers. We are proceeding with caution regarding the potential for mid-game weather interruptions, but the internal projections suggest our lineup depth is well-positioned to capitalize on these specific pitching matchups.",
